WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.484
DAgger collects expert supervision on states visited by the

00:00:03.494 --> 00:00:04.138
robot.

00:00:04.411 --> 00:00:07.031
These new examples join the existing dataset.

00:00:07.304 --> 00:00:09.841
The combined data updates the policy for the next

00:00:09.851 --> 00:00:11.567
round of robot execution.

00:00:12.447 --> 00:00:13.471
But when should help begin?

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:16.055
Here, pickup and handover are familiar.

00:00:16.328 --> 00:00:19.568
The remaining difficulty is precise insertion at a shifted

00:00:19.578 --> 00:00:20.126
target.

00:00:20.988 --> 00:00:24.346
Early takeover can repeat behavior the robot already knows.

00:00:24.631 --> 00:00:27.954
With limited expert data, a smaller share of supervision

00:00:28.036 --> 00:00:30.013
may address the difficult stage.

00:00:30.988 --> 00:00:35.013
With late takeover, the expert demonstration begins after execution

00:00:35.095 --> 00:00:37.656
has deviated from the intended task trajectory.

00:00:37.929 --> 00:00:39.931
It may miss part of the behavior the policy

00:00:39.941 --> 00:00:40.715
needs to learn.

00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:46.276
Timely takeover aims to capture the remaining difficulty from

00:00:46.286 --> 00:00:50.145
a suitable state, concentrating more expert supervision on what

00:00:50.155 --> 00:00:51.918
the policy still needs to learn.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.102
Both early and late interventions can succeed.

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:58.139
But under the same expert-action budget, they can provide

00:00:58.149 --> 00:00:59.543
very different training data.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.519
Who should decide when help begins?

00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:05.638
In human-gated DAgger, a person decides when to intervene.

00:01:05.911 --> 00:01:09.114
In robot-gated DAgger, the robot triggers the request.

00:01:09.387 --> 00:01:12.733
In both cases, the expert still provides the demonstration.

00:01:13.613 --> 00:01:15.996
Can signals inside the policy help choose a useful

00:01:16.006 --> 00:01:16.412
time?

00:01:16.685 --> 00:01:20.449
TimelyDAgger combines internal feature monitoring with feedback from

00:01:20.459 --> 00:01:24.174
completed expert interventions to improve future takeover timing.
